# Decisions log — On AI Assisted Coding

Rationale and reversible choices. `outline.md` stays a plain outline; `PLAN.md`
holds the slide mapping; this file holds the why.

---

## 2026-08-01 · Outline stays an outline

Explanatory prose and diff annotations (`<- changed`, `<- added`) do not belong
in `outline.md`. Decisions live here or in chat.

## 2026-08-01 · Requirements collection is a step, not a preamble

"Once requirements are specified" / "As requirements are specified" were leads
above the list. They are now the loop's first step, "Collect requirements", in
every stage — it is part of the loop, not a precondition to it.

Paired with this: "Until requirements are satisfied" is **not** optional. It is
the loop's general stop condition and carries no `*`. Technical design is now
the only optional step.

Refined further: both are *edges*, not steps. They sit outside the ordered list
(`p.loop-edge.in` / `p.loop-edge.out`, mono `start` / `stop` where the number
would be), so the numbering counts execution steps only. Mirrored in the outline
as `Start:` / `Stop:` lines bracketing each loop's bullets.

Resolved: "Author project specific workflows" is a *third* edge, above `Start:`
— a project-level prerequisite, not a loop step. Labelled `once` in the deck
(`p.loop-edge.pre`), and it carries the `added` mark, since it is what that
stage contributes. So a stage can have a moving part outside the loop.

## 2026-08-01 · Technical design is optional, never dropped

"Technical design of a solution *" is available in almost every loop stage — it
was omitted from some stages in the original outline only to avoid repetition,
not because it disappears.

Consequence: the loops are now normalized. Every stage lists the full skeleton;
`*` marks a step that is optional (always available, not always required).
Nothing is ever removed between stages.

## 2026-08-01 · Stack shows a stable spine, not a +/- diff

Superseded the earlier add/remove treatment (blue `+` rows, coral struck-through
`−` list). With nothing being removed, a diff was the wrong metaphor.

Now: unmarked steps recede to graphite; the one or two steps that actually move
carry a blue left-bar and a mono tag — `added` for a new step, `changed` for a
step that was rewritten. The audience sees one skeleton evolving rather than six
lists.

The moving parts, stage by stage:

| Stage | Moves |
|---|---|
| Traditional | — (establishes the skeleton) |
| AI | *changed*: code, compile → prompt, generate code |
| Autonomous | *changed*: iterate manually → iterate, feed results back into agent |
| TDD | *added*: prompt — generate failing tests |
| Spec driven | *added*: prompt — generate specs |
| Spec driven + workflows | *added*: author project specific workflows |

## 2026-08-01 · "The coding feedback loop" opens the stack

The renamed skeleton section becomes stack slide 0, ahead of Traditional — the
stable components presented once, then only the moving parts iterate. Its build
step is tagged `varies` rather than `added`/`changed`, since it is the slot the
other six stages fill. The three tag words are UI chrome, not outline content.

## 2026-08-01 · No transitions anywhere — text must be crisp on every frame

Superseded the loop-stack-only setting below. The blur was never a theme bug: it
is what animating a transform costs. The browser renders a slide to a texture at
its pre-transform size and resamples that texture while it moves, so display
type softens mid-flight and snaps back at rest. No transition setting avoids it,
because every reveal transition is a transform.

So: `transition: 'none'` and `backgroundTransition: 'none'` deck-wide, the
per-slide `data-transition` attributes removed (one source of truth), and
`transition/animation: none !important` on `.slides` and the sections, since
reveal ships transition declarations regardless of the configured mode.

Slides cut. Nothing moves, nothing is ever resampled.

## 2026-08-01 · (superseded) No transition inside the loop stack

`data-transition="none"` on all seven nested sections. The stack is one diagram
being amended; sliding it vertically implies seven separate things. With the
transition off, the shared rows are pixel-identical between stages and only the
marked row visibly changes — the animation *is* the diff.

Side effect: entering and leaving the stack horizontally is also instant, since
`data-transition` governs a slide's in and out together. Acceptable; the
alternative (`slide-in none-out` on the first) makes reverse navigation slide
sideways into the top of the stack, which is worse.

## 2026-08-01 · Use case is a pinned full-width bar

The use case was a `badge-soft` flowing at the end of the right column, so it
sat at a different height on every stage. It is now `div.use-case`, absolutely
positioned across the full stage above `.slide-foot` — outside `.slide-inner`,
like `.slide-foot` itself, since it is positioned against the section. Mono
`use case` label on the left, the case in 18px ink, and the stage's aside
("You'd need specific skills") pushed to the right edge.

Stage 6 has no use case; it carries the brownfield line under a `context` label
in the same bar.

## 2026-08-01 · Heading is pinned; the list only grows downward

`.slide-inner` centers its content by default, so every stage re-centered the
whole column and the shared rows appeared to drift between stages — which
defeats the point of the no-transition cut. On `.layout-loop`: `justify-content:
flex-start`, `grid-2 { align-items: start }`, and a `min-height: 68px` on the
`d-md` heading so one-line and two-line titles hold the same rail. Steps now
start at the same y on all seven and the list extends downward.

Paid for by dropping the loop slides' top padding to 48px so the widest stage
(10 rows incl. edges) still clears the use-case bar. That is a per-layout
padding override, not a change to the type scale.

## 2026-08-01 · Flattened reveal's 3D stage to stop headings blurring

reveal puts `perspective` on `.slides` and `transform-style: preserve-3d` on the
sections so its cube/page transitions work. Side effect: each slide is
composited as a texture and resampled while it moves, so display type visibly
softens mid-transition. This deck uses slide/fade/none only — none need 3D — so
`.slides { perspective: none }` plus `transform-style: flat` on the sections
puts text back on the normal glyph-rasterisation path.

Cost: the 3D transitions (`cube`, `page`) would no longer work if ever selected.
Acceptable; they are not in this deck's vocabulary.

Correction: the first attempt also set `backface-visibility: hidden`, which made
things worse — it promotes every section to its own composited layer, and the
nested stack sections then sit on a fractional offset and get rasterised once
and scaled. Result: text blurry *while standing still*, and only on the loop
slides, since those are the nested ones. Removed; flattening the 3D context is
enough on its own. There is a comment in the theme warning against re-adding it
or `will-change: transform`.

## 2026-08-01 · Skeleton collapsed into Traditional

The separate "The coding feedback loop" slide was the same list as Traditional
with a placeholder build step. With actor glyphs on every row, Traditional
carries the baseline on its own — all human, nothing marked — so the skeleton
slide was one slide of throat-clearing. Removed from the deck and the outline;
the stack is six stages again, matching the "n of 6" eyebrows.

`assets/loops/loop-00-skeleton.svg` is now unreferenced.

## 2026-08-01 · Actor glyphs: who performs each step

Two `<symbol>`s in a sprite (`#i-human`, `#i-bot`), stroke-only at
`currentColor` so they inherit the row colour and diff treatment. They sit in a
fourth grid column so they hold one rail down the right edge regardless of tag
width, on step rows and loop edges alike.

The arc: Traditional is entirely human; each stage converts its `Prompt —` steps
to the bot, ending with the human authoring only the workflows and the
requirements while every execution step is a bot.

## 2026-08-01 · Loop diagrams replace the card, not fill it

The seven SVGs in `assets/loops/` are wired into the stack. They stand in place
of `.card-feature` rather than inside it — each SVG is already a composed figure
on its own ground, so a card around it only spent 32px of padding a side and
shrank the drawing. The diagram now takes the full left column and centers
against the stage; the steps column stays top-aligned on its rail.

Open: the SVGs predate the refactor step, so stages 4–6 show it in the list but
not in the ring. They also carry their own small "n of 6" counter, which
duplicates the eyebrow.

## 2026-08-01 · Loop stack uses one uniform template

Overrides the reveal skill's "never repeat a layout more than twice in a row"
heuristic, by explicit request: the six stages are the same idea evolving, so
they get the same L-06 two-column frame (diagram left, steps right) in a single
vertical stack. Varying the layout would read as six unrelated topics.

## 2026-08-01 · Loop step list runs one notch tighter

The widest stage carries 9 steps; the theme's default agenda rhythm (20px /
16px padding) overflows 720px there. `ol.agenda.diff` drops to 18px / 11px
padding — applied to all six so row height is identical across the stack.
This is a modifier on the theme's own list, not a new type size.

## 2026-08-01 · Temperature check shows the outline's second level

Sub-items render as a `<span class="sub">` inside the agenda `li`, not a nested
`<ol>` — a nested list restarts the counter and breaks the 56px number rail.

## 2026-08-01 · Deck lives outside the blog's link graph

`presentations/` is not in `_config.yml`'s `exclude:`, so it builds and
publishes at `/blog/presentations/…`, but `index.html` only renders the post
list, so nothing links to it. Assets are relative throughout, which is what
makes it survive `baseurl: "/blog"`.

