## 2026-08-01 · AI stage keeps Traditional's wording; the glyph carries the change

First pass reworded the changed row to "Prompt — generate code". Reverted: the
row now reads "Code, compile", word-for-word what Traditional shows, still
carrying the blue highlight and `changed` tag. The bot glyph is what marks the
row as different now, not the text — so the same activity is visibly the same
line, just performed by a different actor.

Note: `outline.md` still lists this step as "Prompt - generate code" — left
alone, since this was a display-only call. Outline and deck now intentionally
diverge on this one line; flagged to the user.

## 2026-08-01 · Loop headings normalized to "Feedback Loops / <name>"

Headings previously varied in shape ("AI Feedback Loop" one line, "Traditional
Feedback Loop" two lines split before "Feedback Loop", "Spec Driven Loop with
Predefined Workflows" split mid-clause). Now uniform: line one is always
"Feedback Loops", line two is the loop's name only. Synced into outline.md's
section headers (`### Feedback Loops: Traditional`, etc.) so the two documents
group the same way.

Consequence: the eyebrow ("Feedback Loops · N of 6") repeated the now-heading
word, so it was trimmed to just the counter ("N of 6").

## 2026-08-01 · LVL 0-5 added; bot glyph ratchets forward from LVL 1

Each stage's eyebrow now reads "LVL n \u00b7 N of 6" \u2014 LVL 0 Traditional, LVL 1
AI Assisted Coding, LVL 2 Autonomous, LVL 3 TDD, LVL 4 Spec Driven, LVL 5 Spec
Driven + Predefined Workflows. Kept in the eyebrow rather than a standalone
badge (asked directly), accepting that the level and the stack position are
now one string \u2014 if the stack is ever reordered or split, they could drift
apart and would need re-deriving together.

Actor glyphs completed for stages 3\u20136 (Autonomous, TDD, Spec Driven, Spec
Driven + Workflows), which only had them on Traditional and AI before. Rule
applied: the bot glyph ratchets forward and stays \u2014 once a step is bot-
performed at the level that introduces it, every later stage keeps the bot mark
on that same step, whether or not it is that stage's `diff-move` row. Concretely,
only "generate code" carries the bot (from LVL 1 on); every other step, including
newly-added or newly-changed ones (failing tests, refactor, specs, author
workflows), stays human unless a future instruction hands it over too. So a
stage's `changed`/`added` tag and its actor glyph are independent signals now:
one says what\u2019s new, the other says who does it.

LVL 1's heading changed from "AI" to "AI Assisted Coding" for clarity, synced
into outline.md's header too.

## 2026-08-01 · Eyebrow spells out "Level" instead of "LVL"

All six stack slides: "LVL n" -> "Level n" in the eyebrow. Same reasoning as
always spelling out rather than abbreviating in this deck's voice.

## 2026-08-01 · Second bot handoff: Iterate, feed test results back into agent

Level 2 (Autonomous) is where this step's own `changed` tag lives \u2014 it is the
step Autonomous introduces, replacing "Iterate, manually". It now also carries
the bot glyph, and per the same ratchet rule as "Code", the mark carries forward
unmarked through TDD, Spec Driven, and Spec Driven + Workflows (all three
already had this exact row, previously human).

So as of Level 5 there are two automated steps: generate code (from Level 1) and
iterate on test results (from Level 2). Everything else \u2014 generate specs,
generate failing tests, refactor, author workflows \u2014 stays human until a
future instruction hands it over.

## 2026-08-01 · "Code, compile" wording carried through all levels

The "Prompt \u2014 generate code" text was an artifact of no longer having a way to
mark who performs a step; now that the bot glyph does that job, the "Prompt \u2014"
framing was redundant on this row specifically. Applied everywhere it still
said "Prompt \u2014 generate code" (Autonomous, TDD, Spec Driven, Spec Driven +
Workflows) \u2014 all four now read "Code, compile", matching Traditional and AI
Assisted Coding. The SVGs already used a plain "Code" node label, so this also
closes a wording gap between the list and the diagram.

`outline.md` still says "Prompt - generate code" at every level (see the
