## 2026-08-01 · Outline normalized: canonical steps, explicit glyphs, Level tags

The outline is now the single source for the loop progression, structured so the
slides and SVGs can be annotated mechanically from it.

Canonical step vocabulary, in fixed order. A level carries every step that has
been introduced at or before it; nothing is ever dropped, only added:

  once   Author project specific workflows   L5
  start  Collect requirements                all
   1     Generate specs                      L4+
   2     Technical design of a solution *    all
   3     Generate failing tests              L3+
   4     Code                                all
   5     Run                                 all
   6     Test                                all
   7     Iterate                             all
   8     Refactor                            L3+
  stop   Until requirements are satisfied    all

Three actors, explicit on every step (no "unmarked means human" default) so the
outline maps 1:1 to the slide rows, which already draw a glyph per row:
:human:, :bot:, :laptop:. Run is :laptop: at every level including Traditional —
it was always the computer executing; it is not a human step that gets taken
over, which is why it never appears in the ratchet.

Bot ratchet — a step flips at the level that introduces the change and stays
flipped downstream: Code at L1, Test + Iterate at L2, Generate failing tests at
L3, Generate specs + Refactor at L4, Technical design at L5.

Dropped the "Prompt - " prefix everywhere (generate specs / generate failing
tests / refactor). Same reasoning as the earlier "Code, compile" fix: the prefix
was standing in for who performs the step, and the glyph now does that job.
This also closes the outline-vs-deck divergence flagged on 2026-08-01 — the
outline's "Prompt - generate code" is now plain "Code" at every level.

Wording collapsed to one form per step: "Test" (was also "Test, get feedback"),
"Iterate" (was also "Iterate, manually" and "Iterate, feed test results back
into agent"), "Code" (was also "Prompt - generate code"), "Refactor" (was also
"Prompt - refactor").

Reverted "Start: Collect requirements :bot:" at L4 to :human: — it contradicted
the stated endpoint, where the human authors only workflows and requirements.

Judgment calls made while normalizing, flagged for review:
  - L3 Refactor is :human: and flips to :bot: at L4. TDD's refactor step is the
    one a developer drives; spec-driven hands it over.
  - L5 Technical design flips to :bot: — the workflows encode the design
    conventions, so the agent applies them. This is the last human execution
    step to go, which is what makes L5 the end of the progression.
  - L5 gained "Use case: brownfield projects"; it was the only level without one.

Formatting: Once/Start/Stop hug the step list (they are loop edges, not numbered
steps), blank line before "Use case:".

Not yet propagated: index.html has only #i-human and #i-bot in its sprite and
needs a third :laptop: symbol, plus a Run-row and Technical-design-row update at
several levels. The SVGs are likewise unannotated.

## 2026-08-01 · Outline changes propagated into the deck HTML

Added `#i-laptop` to the actor sprite and `.actor.machine` for it. First pass
coloured it `--gp-graphite` (#636363), which is *darker* than the human glyph's
`--gp-steel` (#c2c2c2) on this canvas — so Run, the one step that never changes
hands, read as the loudest mark on the row. Corrected to `--gp-fog`: Run is
background machinery and belongs below human in prominence, not above. Also
stopped `li.diff-move .actor` from recolouring a machine glyph blue — a machine
glyph is never the thing that changed.

Merged "Collect requirements" and "Generate specs" into one edge step at L4 and
L5: from Level 4 on the spec *is* the requirements, so keeping them apart was
double-counting one activity. The merged edge reads "Collect requirements,
generate specs" and carries two glyphs, human + bot — the first genuinely
collaborative step in the deck. This is now L4's own marked change (`changed`
on the edge) rather than an `added` numbered step, and it drops L4 and L5 to
8 and 9 rows, easing the density problem on the widest slide.

Two actors on one row needed a `.actors` flex wrapper and the glyph column
widened 24px -> 44px on both `.loop-edge` and `ol.agenda.diff li`, right-aligned
so the single-actor rows keep the same rail.

Verified by screenshot at 1280x720 on levels 0, 4, 5: glyphs render, three
colours are distinguishable, L5 fits with room to spare.

Still out of sync: the SVG diagrams. They carry a separate "Specs" node, and
node labels ("Fail tests", "Feed back") predate the normalized vocabulary. No
diagram shows actors at all.

## 2026-08-01 · Flagged every actor handover; "Specify requirements"; brownfield folded in

Three actor handovers were unflagged: Test at L2, Refactor at L4, Technical
design at L5. Only the *first* of a level's changes had been marked. Rule now:
a step is flagged `changed` at the level where its actor changes, regardless of
how many other rows on that slide also changed. L2 therefore carries two flags
(Test and Iterate), which is the honest reading of "Autonomous" — the agent
takes over both testing and iterating in one step.

Renamed the start edge to "Specify requirements" at all six levels. "Collect
requirements, generate specs" was accurate after the merge but too long for the
row; the shorter name covers both halves and keeps the edge to one line.

Two layout bugs the flags exposed, both on L5's design row — the only row that
carries two tags (`optional` + `changed`):
  1. `ol.agenda.diff li` had a single tag column, so the second tag auto-placed
     into the glyph column and pushed the actor onto a second line. Now two auto
     tag columns. This is the same failure the existing comment already warned
     about for one tag; it recurred at two.
  2. That widening then wrapped the label. Fixed by taking the glyph column back
     from 44px to 24px on numbered rows — only a *loop edge* ever carries two
     glyphs — and right-aligning every glyph so edges and steps still share one
     rail.

Folded the "Brownfield Projects" section into Level 5 rather than keeping it as
its own outline section: it existed to introduce a context that is now just this
loop's use case. The `.use-case.brownfield::before { content: "context" }`
variant is gone, so all six bars read "use case". "Brownfield projects have more
context baked in" survives as the slide's uc-note, the same slot L2 and L3 use.

## 2026-08-01 · Pinned the loop edges so the numbered list never moves

Level 5's pre-loop "once" row pushed its start edge and whole numbered list down
relative to the other five, so paging through the stack made the list jump. The
other five now reserve that slot with an invisible copy of the row
(`.loop-edge.pre.spacer`, `visibility: hidden`, `aria-hidden`) rather than a
hand-computed offset — the edges are <p> elements carrying block margins on top
of row padding and a border, so the arithmetic is not just row height and would
rot the moment any of those change.

The spacer carries the same children as the real row (tag span + actor glyph),
not just the label: a bare text line boxes 2px shorter than one containing an
11px mono tag and a 17px svg.

Separately, the paired-glyph `.actors` wrapper needed `align-self: center` to
match a bare `.actor`. Under the grid's `align-items: baseline` the wrapper's
baseline differs from a lone svg's, which dropped L4 and L5 by 2px — visible
only when measured, but it defeated the point of pinning.

Verified by measuring text-row tops off the screenshots rather than by eye: all
six levels now put the start edge at y=227 and step 01 at y=278, with L5's once
row at y=176.

Note for future screenshot runs: Chrome blocks port 5060 (ERR_UNSAFE_PORT) and
silently captures an error page, which reads as a plausible-looking measurement.
Use a port outside Chrome's blocked list.

## 2026-08-01 · Pinned the stop edge too

Same argument as the start edge: the two edges bracket the loop, so the steps
should grow *into* the frame rather than push its bottom down. Implemented as a
`min-height` on `.layout-loop ol.agenda.diff` sized to Level 5's eight-step list
(244px); shorter stages leave the remainder empty. All six now put start at
y=227, stop at y=522.

First attempt used 284px — derived from row-height arithmetic rather than the
measured list — and pushed stop to 562 on every slide, i.e. consistently wrong
rather than obviously wrong. Measured the real list height instead. Worth
remembering: with a pin like this, an error is uniform across all slides and so
looks like the intended design.

Cost: Level 0 has ~100px of empty space between "Iterate" and the stop line, and
Levels 1-2 the same. Read as "room the later levels fill", which supports the
progression; read uncharitably, as a layout bug. Reversible by deleting the one
min-height rule.

Correction, same day: replaced the `min-height` pin with hidden `li.spacer` rows
padding each short list to the longest one (seven steps). Same rendered result —
start y=227, stop y=522 on all six — but no pixel constant to go stale, and a
level that gains a step later simply needs one fewer spacer instead of a
recomputed height. This is the same technique as the pre-edge spacer, so both
pins now work the same way.

Two details that make it safe: a hidden row still increments the step counter,
so spacers only ever go at the end of a list; and `visibility: hidden` hides the
row's hairline separator too, so the reserved space reads as clean white rather
than a run of empty ruled rows.

The max step count is seven, not eight as the min-height attempt assumed —
another reason the measured/structural approach beats the arithmetic one.

## 2026-08-01 · One background colour for the whole deck

Exactly one slide was off: Temperature Check carried `s-cloud` (#f7f7f7) while
the other eight were `s-canvas` (#ffffff) — enough to read as a flicker when
paging past it, not enough to look intentional. Now `s-canvas` throughout;
sampled every slide and the only non-white pixels left are the title slide's
blue chevrons, which are artwork rather than a band.

This drops the design system's canvas -> cloud -> fog -> ink band rhythm, which
the skill treats as the section-boundary signal. Deliberate: this deck carries
its structure in the loop stack and the Level eyebrows, and a single surface
keeps the six loop slides reading as one continuous topic. If dividers or a
closing ink slab get added later, revisit — the rhythm is worth more once there
are sections to separate.

## 2026-08-01 · Both loop edges become collaborative

The stop condition ("Until requirements are satisfied") takes the bot glyph
alongside the human from Level 4 on, and authoring the workflows is human + bot
at Level 5. Flagged `changed` at L4 where the stop actor first changes; carried
unflagged at L5, per the ratchet.

Effect on the argument: at Level 4 both edges — specify requirements and the
stop condition — are human + bot, so the loop is now *bracketed* by
collaboration rather than merely containing it. The human no longer hands work
over and waits; they judge "done" together with the agent. That is a stronger
end state than "every execution step is a bot", and it is the one the deck now
builds to.

Level 5's authoring row keeps its `added` flag and gains the pair, so the only
purely human marks left anywhere in the stack are gone — every row is bot,
laptop, or human+bot.

The five pre-edge spacers were updated to carry the paired-glyph wrapper too:
they exist to mirror the real row's height, so they have to track its structure,
not just its text. Re-measured after the change — start y=227, stop y=522 on all
six, unchanged.

Also removed a stray blank line before L4's `Stop:` in outline.md; every level
now brackets its list the same way.

## 2026-08-01 · Refactor arrives collaborative at L3, goes bot-only at L4

Refactor now enters as human + bot at Level 3 (still tagged `added`) and becomes
bot-only at Level 4 (tagged `changed`). Previously it entered human-only, which
overstated how much of TDD's refactor step a person actually drives.

This gives the deck a second three-beat handover — appears collaborative, then
goes fully to the agent — matching the shape the requirements/spec edge already
has, rather than every step flipping human -> bot in a single move.

First numbered row to carry two glyphs, so the paired-glyph wrapper needed a
grid column inside `ol.agenda.diff li`, and that row alone needs the wide 44px
glyph column. Scoped with `li:has(.actors)` rather than widening every row —
each row is its own grid, so the wide column would otherwise squeeze the labels
on rows that carry two tags (which is what wrapped L5's design row earlier).
Both variants right-align, so the glyph rail still reads as one column.

Re-measured: start y=227, stop y=522 on all six.

## 2026-08-01 · SVGs: regenerate from a script, on fixed slots

The six diagrams were authored against a much older step list and had drifted
past the point where patching made sense: `Feed back` where the list says
`Iterate`, `Fail tests` where it says `Failing tests`, a `Specs` node that no
longer exists as a step, no `Refactor` node at all, and no actor badges in the
deck's vocabulary. Levels 3-5 each needed a node added, a node removed, and the
whole circle re-spaced.

Decided to write `assets/loops/generate.py` holding the step/actor spec for all
six levels and emit the SVGs from it, rather than hand-patch six files. One pass
instead of six, geometry identical across diagrams by construction, and the next
outline change is a one-line edit plus a re-run. (The README already claimed a
generate.py existed; now it will be true.)

Layout: seven fixed anchor positions around the circle — design, failing tests,
code, run, test, iterate, refactor — used by every level, with absent steps
leaving their slot empty. Nodes therefore never move between slides: paging
through shows steps appearing in place rather than the loop rotating. Same
principle as the hidden spacer rows in the step list, applied to the circle.

Spec and the agent prompt live in `svg-prompts.md`.

