## 2026-08-02 · Part 2 intro diagram — the three timelines

Experimental slide, sits after the Part 2 ink divider and before "how to add X".
Brainstorming only — deliberately NOT synced to `outline.md` yet.

Generated by `assets/context/generate.py` → `assets/context/timelines.svg`, same
generator convention as `assets/loops`.

Three lanes, left to right, each ending at the same goal on the right edge. A
dotted steel line marks the ideal straight run start-to-goal, so deviation is
measurable by eye rather than asserted. Straight polyline segments, not curves:
each joint is a hard corner, so the line reads as a path taken rather than a
smooth trajectory.

**The axis is WHEN THE CORRECTION WAS AUTHORED, not who applies it.** This was
the correction that made the slide work. The first labels ("manual corrections /
self-applied corrections / workflow") named the wrong axis and made the three
lanes look like a restatement of each other. What actually varies:

- Lane 1 "Correct as you go" — authored during the run, reactively. Nothing
  exists before the run starts.
- Lane 2 "Plan first, then execute" — authored at the start of the run. Fires as
  the run passes each checkpoint, but single-use and dies with the feature.
- Lane 3 "Reusable workflow" — authored before the run and reused across runs:
  stencils, checkers, spec generators.

This maps onto Part 1's levels, which is the hand-off Part 2 needs.

Consequences of naming that axis correctly:

- Lane 2's four remaining purple dots are the *argument for* lane 3, not a
  blemish — a plan authored up front does not survive contact with the run.
- Lane 3's single purple dot at the start is the strongest mark on the slide:
  the human sets the goal, and it is the only thing they do.

**Glyphs carry one meaning each and it does not shift between lanes** — that is
what makes the lanes comparable:

- purple dot — unplanned human intervention. Gets rarer down the lanes.
- blue diamond — a checkpoint from a plan. NOT a self-applied correction; that
  was the original misreading.
- dotted ring — a pre-authored step. The dotted, empty treatment says "template,
  not instance", which is what a reusable step is.

Purple (`#6E3FD1`) is NOT in the design system and is confined to human
intervention — dots and the step rings. Everything else is a theme token: blue
diamonds `--gp-primary`, line `--gp-ink`, ideal line and goal ring `--gp-steel`.
Adding purple to the palette is still an open design-system decision.

Open: lane 3 carries both diamonds and rings, i.e. "pre-authored steps plus the
plan's checkpoints". Believed correct — the workflow generates the plan, so both
layers exist — but it is the one place the slide asks the audience to hold two
ideas at once.

Craft notes worth not re-litigating:
- Self/plan checkpoint spacing is deliberately irregular (gaps 12–62px, runs of
  two or three drifting the same way before turning, amplitudes 2–30px). Strict
  alternation reads as a sawtooth waveform — a mechanical rhythm — instead of
  corrections landing where the run needs them.
- No lane may place a correction on top of the goal; lane 3's step cadence is
  148px specifically so six steps fit and still end clear of the ring.
- Lane pitch is 146px (lanes at y 92/238/384) because tighter pitch collided the
  lane labels with the previous lane's lowest dot.

