## 2026-08-03 — Implementation and Validation stages, drilled one step at a time

Same structure as Requirements: a stage-goal framing line plus an approach note
per row. Content came from asking one question per step.

Implementation — goal: working code that satisfies the requirements.
- "Technical notes" is really **Technical design**: requirements plus repo
  access produce an outline of the code changes, i.e. a design document. Now
  matches the loop slides' "technical design of a solution".
- **Plan** sequences the work and is where you opt into TDD — red/green, or
  red/green/blue with the refactor beat.
- **Code** added; nothing named the build itself once Infra and Stencils left.
  Named "Code" rather than "Execute" so the vocabulary matches Part 1.
- **Refactor** is both: the blue beat inside each cycle, and one pass across
  the finished slice.
- **Infra** removed. It is an optional step interchangeable with a service code
  change — same loop, different target — so it moved to Part 3 "Running wider"
  (now a three-up) rather than sitting in the main sequence.
- **Stencils** moved to speaker notes. It is not a step in the loop; it is what
  you build *because* the loop repeats — stencils for unit tests, integration
  tests, endpoints, controllers, resolvers.

QA renamed **Validation** and nested as Implementation's last step, because the
agent needs it to iterate: it feeds the coding loop rather than following it. It
keeps its own slide so the four checks are not crowded into the Implementation
table — the slide reads as zooming into that one step. Row: "Validation — the
last step, and what the agent iterates against"; slide framing: "The last step of
implementation, up close."

Validation rows: End to end (curl/Playwright — a way to exercise the *running*
system), Test tiers (review requirements, pick the right tier per requirement —
not everything needs a unit test, not everything needs e2e — and feed the plan
back as notes), Review cycle (different model, against the requirements, in CI),
Drift check (gate before a PR is merge-ready; requirements are tracked in code
now, so it runs in CI and inside the loop).

