## 2026-08-04 — Restructured into Requirements, Planning, Implementation

The old Requirements / Implementation / Validation split was wrong in two ways.
Validation contained planning work (deciding test tiers is a planning decision,
not a verification one), and implementation was sitting in the middle when the
loop it describes is what everything else feeds into. Now:

**Requirements** — unchanged. Goal: clear requirements, functional and non
functional.

**Planning** (new) — goal: steps the agent can run unattended, with checkpoints
to validate progress. Technical design, testing plan (verifies against the
requirements), implementation plan (verified against the tests), testing tiers
review. Note the verification chain: requirements verify the tests, tests verify
the code plan. That ordering is the reason planning is its own stage.

**Implementation loop** — goal: working code that satisfies the requirements.
Seven steps, three of them Code: implement the tests, make them pass, refactor
while they stay passing. Then review (another model, iterate the loop),
validation (end to end, then iterate or submit proof of work), drift check
(code/requirements/checks triplet), and All green → merge. Uses table.cmp.dense
since seven rows are past plain .cmp's comfortable cap.

The old standalone Validation slide is gone; its four rows redistributed —
test tiers to Planning, review cycle and drift check into the loop, end to end
into the validation step.

Closing the stack: an L-04 statement that the agent can fall back to human
control at any stage, when requirements were too ambiguous or scope creep got
into the loop or was implied in the original spec. It applies to all three
stages, so a row on any single table would have understated it.

## 2026-08-04 — Implementation loop: three Code rows collapse to one

"Implement the tests / Make the tests pass / Refactor" are one red-green-blue
turn, not three steps of the loop — splitting them made the TDD cycle look like
a third of the loop's weight when the other four rows are each a distinct beat.
Now one row: "Implement the tests, implement the code for those tests, then
refactor keeping the tests green."

Five rows fits plain table.cmp again, so .dense came back off and the row rhythm
matches the Requirements and Planning tables.

## 2026-08-04 — Helpful links: descriptions, URL under the name

The table was a name and a URL, which told the audience nothing about why any of
the three is worth opening. Second column is now "What it is"; the URL moved
under the project name in small mono (new .link rule in the theme, scoped to
table.cmp td).

Reordered so the two spec driven frameworks sit together and groundcrew, which
is a different kind of tool, comes last. Its description is also the longest,
so it reads better at the bottom than wedged between two short ones.

spec-kit and conductor are both "a reusable spec driven framework". Two identical
cells read as a copy-paste error, so conductor's says "The same, packaged as a
Gemini CLI extension" — a distinction taken from its own URL rather than invented.

## 2026-08-04 — "Pre-built prompts and skills" becomes "Workflow"

The old title named the artifacts; the new one names the thing: a predefined set
of steps to follow during implementation. That is also what the three cards under
